\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Finite evidence for the synthesis conjecture}
\date{2026-10-30}

\p{The [synthesis conjecture](fdip-003X) connects discrete induction
to arithmetic, symmetry, backgrounds, continuum limits and physical
observables. The [opposite-vertex elimination](fdip-000F) supplies a
concrete finite comparison: averaging the removed fields preserves
retained expectations, while fixing those fields can change them.
The following eight comparisons distinguish such proved constructions
from the additional identifications required by each part of the
conjecture.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Arithmetic scheme.} The
[arithmetic four-cycle family](fdip-000U) gives an explicit coefficient
ring and separate open domains for a nondegenerate form, an invertible
eliminated block and a trace observable. The [bad fibers](fdip-000W)
show why these domains differ. This constructs a family; it does not
select a unique arithmetic model for a physical system.}
\li{\strong{Clifford sheaf.} The
[configuration Clifford algebra](fdip-000P) and its
[form-preserving maps](fdip-000Q) extend to a
[sheaf compatible with scalar extension](fdip-000V).
For the [rational rank-four forms](fdip-0041),
[Schur elimination](fdip-0044) multiplies the Galois characters
of the retained and eliminated even centers. Primitive Dirichlet
representatives must be formed after this product; multiplying their
zero extensions need not give the same function, and no L-function
multiplication rule follows.
A physical spin or matter interpretation and an analytic coupling to
observables require further maps. Configuration Clifford algebras and
[spatial Clifford modules](fdip-000R) are distinct objects.}
\li{\strong{Finite-group action.} The specified
[half-turn](fdip-000J), [group-algebra action](fdip-003N) and
[sector marginals](fdip-003R) make symmetry and Gaussian elimination
compatible under their stated hypotheses. The
[cycle half-turn](fdip-0045) fixes the even Clifford center pointwise;
the nontrivial algebra involution of that center is a different action.
These supplied symmetries do not select a physical gauge group or
its dynamics.}
\li{\strong{Backgrounds and moduli.} The
[enriched background groupoid](fdip-000X) specifies objects, arrows,
reference pairings and retained projections.
[Scalar descent](fdip-000Y) preserves chosen invariants, while
[quadratic isometry](fdip-000Z) can forget essential background data.
A groupoid of this kind alone supplies neither descent over a site nor
a physical environment-selection map.}
\li{\strong{Continuum comparison.} The
[finite spatial Dirac calculation](fdip-000S) and its
[zero modes and nearest-neighbor comparison](fdip-000T) identify
finite spectra and an operator mismatch. They do not supply an
indexed-family convergence theorem for induced observables or a map
from moduli controlling that limit.}
\li{\strong{Parameters and L-values.} The
[trace ratio](fdip-003H) is an independently defined finite observable;
its [cycle values](fdip-000I) distinguish backgrounds.
[Two rational fibers](fdip-0043) have the same quadratic center
character and different trace ratios. The
[fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042) therefore excludes recovering
this ratio from any fixed collection of L-values determined solely by
that character on this family. This conclusion concerns the stated coarse invariant;
no physical parameter is identified and richer arithmetic invariants
remain outside the claim.}
\li{\strong{Discrete induction.} The
[Gaussian elimination theorem](fdip-000D),
[retained expectation](fdip-000E) and
[cycle calculation](fdip-000F) construct an effective precision,
normalization factor and new couplings, with
[symmetry transport](fdip-000H). A
[formal quartic interaction](fdip-0046) composes under elimination
through second order in its coupling and transports a supplied
symmetry. The [three-coordinate example](fdip-0047) exhibits constant,
quadratic, quartic and sextic terms and computes a normalized connected
four-point function; [stationary elimination](fdip-0048) separates
tree contributions from fluctuation terms. These finite formal
calculations do not reconstruct eliminated data or supply a
nonperturbative theory.}
\li{\strong{Physical equations and observables.} These finite
operators and normalized laws form a mathematical foundation.
A comparison with Standard Model, quantum-field or general-relativistic
equations still requires specified dynamics and observables, including
the relevant gauge, matter, time and gravitational structures.
No such physical identification is established by the finite identities
above.}
}
